  "query_preview": "..\n\nLarge-scale troughing over the eastern US will continue to leave us \nunder subsidence, with high pressure dominating the forecast through \nthe first half of the weekend. This pattern will lead to continued \ndry conditions and comfortable, near-normal temperatures. Shortwave \nactivity embedded within northwest flow aloft tracks south of the \narea Friday morning through Friday evening. While the forecast \nremains dry, areas along and south of M-59 will likely see an uptick \nin cloud cover. \n\nThe forecast looks to remain dry through much of Saturday, with the \npattern turning more active for late Saturday night and into next \nweek as a developing upper-level ridge becomes highly amplified \nacross the central CONUS and strong northwest flow develops aloft. \nBeing on the periphery of the upper-level ridge, we will see an \nuptick in warm air and moisture advection into the region. Both \nSunday and Monday, 925-850 mb temperatures will support temperatures \nin the upper 80s, perhaps a few spots approaching 90 degrees. Dew \npoint temperatures will also be on the increase as moisture moves in \nfrom the southwest, helping to push heat index values a few degrees \nhigher than air temperatures.\n\nThe big question for the latter half of the weekend and into early \nnext week are chances for strong to severe thunderstorms. As the \nheat and humidity build back into the region on the periphery of the \nupper-level ridge, we will need to keep an eye out for where \nthunderstorm activity initiates and tracks across the region. Medium \nrange guidance continue to hint at numerous rounds of organized \nconvective activity tracking from northwest to southeast across the \nwestern Great Lake region. At this time, southeast Michigan remains \non the gradient of better instability building into the region and \nmore than enough deep-layer shear for organized severe weather. \nThus, we will need to keep a close eye the severe weather forecast \nfor Sunday through Monday night. Looking at various AI/ML tools for \nSunday, a few do highlight some of southeast Michigan for possible \nsevere weather. Then for Monday, the SPC has southeast Michigan \nunder a 15% Day 5 Outlook and a few AI/ML tools show the potential \nfor even higher probabilities.",
